WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:02.990
SOFTWARE ENGINEERS:
By now, I think we’ve established that software

00:00:02.990 --> 00:00:07.280
engineers at FAANG companies are paid a boat
load of money.

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:14.820
This is usually anywhere between $175,000
per year to as much as $1.82 million per year.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:19.960
While it’s extraordinarily difficult to
get to level 7 or 8 and earn the 7 figure

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:24.430
salaries, it’s relatively possible to get
to level 5 or 6 if you make the correct education

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:28.000
and careers choices and are willing to grind
out leetcode.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:34.010
But, all of this comes with one caveat, you
have to go into software engineering.

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:39.450
From an objective perspective, this choice
seems like a no brainer, but not everyone

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:44.190
is good at software engineering, and even
if they are, they don’t necessarily want

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:46.230
to surf stackoverflow all day.

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:51.170
They’d much rather talk with customers and
colleagues, set business goals, and drive

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:52.850
forward the company.

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:57.719
Fortunately, while software engineers get
the highest salaries, there are several roles

00:00:57.719 --> 00:01:01.739
at these companies that pay nearly as much
with no coding experience.

00:01:01.739 --> 00:01:07.770
So, here’s the top 3 highest paying FAANG
jobs that don’t require coding.

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:12.850
PRODUCT MANAGERS:
Starting off with one of the broadest jobs

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:16.229
offered at FAANG, we have product managers.

00:01:16.229 --> 00:01:20.280
Product managers are responsible for setting
the goals and vision of a given product and

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.450
coordinating the right people.

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:27.049
For example, if you were the product manager
of Instagram, your first goal would be to

00:01:27.049 --> 00:01:29.860
define the needs of Instagram users.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:35.170
For Instagram, this would probably be hours
of mindless entertainment along with social

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:36.170
validation.

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:39.970
The second step would be to create different
pathways in which your product can achieve

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:41.159
these goals.

00:01:41.159 --> 00:01:45.440
One pathway might be to leverage photos while
another would be to leverage short form video.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:50.380
Once you’ve got a couple of strong plans,
your job would be to convince senior leadership

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:55.770
that they should actually put resources behind
these plans and make them a reality.

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:59.710
If all goes well, you’ll have to work with
technical people to make sure that your vision

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:01.630
is actually executed correctly.

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:06.490
In the end, you’re performance will be evaluated
based on how well you achieved the needs of

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:08.840
your customers and stakeholders.

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:13.670
For example, how much did your idea increase
user engagement or how much did it increase

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:19.569
user satisfaction.

00:02:19.569 --> 00:02:24.011
As you can see, there’s nothing that’s
technically difficult about this role, but

00:02:24.011 --> 00:02:26.900
that’s also makes the job so difficult.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:32.050
You’re often working with abstract concepts
and visions to produce quantifiable results.

00:02:32.050 --> 00:02:37.180
On the brightside, while the title has the
word manager in it, you wont have any direct

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:40.610
reports unless you’re at an extremely senior
level.

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:45.030
This means that you can be hired into this
role straight out of college as an L3 product

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:46.030
manager.

00:02:46.030 --> 00:02:50.140
And most of the people at this level are indeed
fresh college grads.

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:54.580
You definitely wont be working on something
as influential as Instagram or YouTube at

00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:59.400
that level, but you may get to work on certain
subproducts of these systems.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:04.870
For example, you may be in charge of Instagram’s
newsfeed or YouTube’s like button.

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:09.379
In terms of career trajectory, you have the
potential to reach 7 figures as a product

00:03:09.379 --> 00:03:11.970
director, so there’s plenty of room to grow.

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:17.330
Also, most C suite executives were product
managers at one point in their careers including

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:18.330
Sundar Pichai.

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:22.200
So, you’re if you’re aiming for the moon,
this would be the track for you.

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:27.000
But, we should note that this is one of the
hardest careers to break into simply because

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.799
its so abstract and broad.

00:03:29.799 --> 00:03:34.799
Virtually anyone can apply to this role and
meet the minimum qualifications, but it’s

00:03:34.799 --> 00:03:39.400
extremely difficult to actually get an interview
and then convince the interviewer that you’re

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:40.400
the right choice.

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:45.239
Also, a lot of the products that you work
on will be experimental meaning that they’ll

00:03:45.239 --> 00:03:47.340
be the first to get cut during a recession.

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:54.730
But, if you’re looking for a sort of entrepreneurial
leadership role, this would be the way to

00:03:54.730 --> 00:03:55.730
go.

00:03:55.730 --> 00:03:59.849
PROGRAM MANAGERS:
Moving onto a strongly related but starkly

00:03:59.849 --> 00:04:02.689
different role, we have program managers.

00:04:02.689 --> 00:04:07.560
Again, while the title has the word “manager”
it it, you probably wont be managing people

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:09.629
unless you’re L7 or beyond.

00:04:09.629 --> 00:04:13.540
Instead, you’re role would be to manage
programs.

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:18.640
Your primary job as a program manager would
be the execute the vision of the product manager.

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:24.430
If the product manager was the CEO of a product,
you would essentially be the COO.

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:28.580
You would need to ensure that products are
delivered on time and within the budget.

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:33.169
For example, if the product manager wants
a search feature that can scale to 100 million

00:04:33.169 --> 00:04:38.090
users, it will be your job to make sure that
the backend systems can keep up in a cost

00:04:38.090 --> 00:04:39.660
effective manner.

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:43.570
This generally means working with software
engineers and solutions architects to create

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:45.720
robust scalable programs.

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:50.310
Basically, you would be responsible for bringing
together all of the details to make the product

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:52.290
managers vision a reality.

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:57.600
There are two main types of program managers
which are just program managers and technical

00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:58.680
program managers.

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:03.460
If you’re looking into this role, I think
you definitely want to go the technical program

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:05.570
manager or TPM route.

00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:09.820
Not only does the technical route pay substantially
more, but it’s much easier to score one

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:13.450
of these roles if you do indeed have the technical
knowhow.

00:05:13.450 --> 00:05:18.759
One option for TPMs is to become a software
engineering TPM, but there are plenty of TPM

00:05:18.759 --> 00:05:21.960
roles that don’t require any software engineering
skills.

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:27.889
Instead, they require technical knowledge
of cloud systems, databases, networks, enterprise

00:05:27.889 --> 00:05:32.130
solutions like SAP, Oracle, Salesforce and
so on.

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:38.240
While it does take time to learn these disciplines,
you often become irreplaceable within an organization.

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:42.420
During a market downturn, it’s easy for
companies to slash products and thus slash

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:45.310
product managers and regular program managers.

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:50.759
But, it’s way more difficult to cut a technical
program manager whos responsible for Google

00:05:50.759 --> 00:05:55.040
Cloud or essential internal software like
SAP S4 Hana.

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:59.440
So, being a technical program manager gives
you solid mix of having technical skills that

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:04.080
are hard to replace but also not having to
do technical stuff all day.

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:08.110
If you chose the regular program manager route
on the other hand, you’d be doing essentially

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:12.569
the same thing, except you likely wont be
dealing with tech products.

00:06:12.569 --> 00:06:18.389
Instead, you’ll probably be dealing with
the supply chain, marketing or UI.

00:06:18.389 --> 00:06:22.900
For example, if you were a supply chain program
manager at Apple, you may be responsible for

00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:27.080
ensuring that enough iPhones make their way
to retail locations in the southern part of

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:28.099
the US.

00:06:28.099 --> 00:06:32.880
Similarly, if you were a UI program manager,
you may be responsible for testing various

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:36.130
UI features and delivering the most effective
set of features.

00:06:36.130 --> 00:06:39.860
You should keep in mind though that if you’re
targeting one of these broader roles though,

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:45.259
you’ll likely be facing a lot more competition
simply because more people will meet the requirements.

00:06:45.259 --> 00:06:49.750
This is yet another reason to go down the
technical route, but in the end, it’s up

00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:51.500
to you.

00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:56.990
SOLUTIONS ARCHITECT:
Moving onto the next role, we have my personal

00:06:56.990 --> 00:06:59.660
favorite which is solutions architect.

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:04.120
Unlike program manager and product manager
roles that appeal a broad range of people,

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:07.600
solutions architect roles are usually extremely
specific.

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:12.190
These roles are very technical meaning that
you can’t BS your way into one of these

00:07:12.190 --> 00:07:15.479
roles, but that’s also the beauty of these
roles.

00:07:15.479 --> 00:07:20.680
You’ll only be competing with people who
also know the technical stuff, but what exactly

00:07:20.680 --> 00:07:22.780
does a solutions architect do?

00:07:22.780 --> 00:07:27.890
Well, one way to think about it is that they’re
basically the software engineers of none coding

00:07:27.890 --> 00:07:28.930
tasks.

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:33.970
For example, if you need to code an algorithm,
you would contact a software engineer, but

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:38.280
if you need to deploy that algorithm onto
the cloud at scale, you would contact a cloud

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:39.370
solutions architect.

00:07:39.370 --> 00:07:44.960
That’s definitely an oversimplication of
the role, and there are some solutions architects

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:46.710
that need to know coding.

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:51.210
But in general, solutions architects handle
all of the technological tasks that don’t

00:07:51.210 --> 00:07:52.389
involve coding.

00:07:52.389 --> 00:07:58.229
This includes things such as migrations, deployments,
database management, network management, cloud

00:07:58.229 --> 00:08:00.169
infrastructure, and IT security.

00:08:00.169 --> 00:08:03.870
Usually, you’ll choose one sector to be
an expert in.

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:09.310
For example, you can be an expert in cloud
solutions and know the ins and outs of AWS,

00:08:09.310 --> 00:08:11.680
GCP, and Azure.

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:16.979
Or you can be an expert in enterprise solutions
and know everything about Oracle, SAP, and

00:08:16.979 --> 00:08:18.470
Salesforce.

00:08:18.470 --> 00:08:23.009
Given the technical nature of the role, job
security is usually on par with software engineers

00:08:23.009 --> 00:08:24.940
if not even more.

00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:29.370
This is especially true if you specialize
in something like cyber security.

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:33.469
Just because the job requires a bunch of technical
knowledge doesn’t mean that you’ll be

00:08:33.469 --> 00:08:35.700
doing technical stuff all day though.

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:40.570
Much of the job has to do with conveying technical
information to non technical people.

00:08:40.570 --> 00:08:44.480
For instance, if you’re an internal solutions
architect, you would likely need to work with

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:48.880
a bunch of internal technical program managers
and provide them with the best backend solutions

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:50.480
for a given product.

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:54.149
If you’re an external solutions architect,
you’ll work with enterprise customers and

00:08:54.149 --> 00:08:57.600
help these customers implement your company’s
solutions to the best extent.

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:02.850
If you were a solutions architect at AWS for
example, your job might to be keep Netflix

00:09:02.850 --> 00:09:09.160
happy in terms of the backend.

00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:16.191
In terms of career trajectory, solutions architects
don’t quite make it into 7 figures, but

00:09:16.191 --> 00:09:19.160
that’s simply because there’s not as many
levels.

00:09:19.160 --> 00:09:23.440
In terms of the compensation at each level
though, you basically get the same compensation

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:24.630
as a TPM.

00:09:24.630 --> 00:09:29.990
For example, an L7 solutions architect at
Google makes $535 while an L7 TPM at Google

00:09:29.990 --> 00:09:30.990
makes $527.

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:36.060
Also, if you’re a star solutions architect,
you’ll have an easy time switching to senior

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:37.570
TPM roles.

00:09:37.570 --> 00:09:42.440
For example, a reasonable switch may be to
go from being an L7 enterprise architect to

00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:47.700
being the director of enterprise resource
planning which basically an L8 TPM role.

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:53.360
From there, you can become a managing director,
VP, Senior VP and so on and so forth.

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:57.540
So, in the end, if you’re willing to do
technical stuff but don’t want necessarily

00:09:57.540 --> 00:10:03.420
want to code, solutions architect is definitely
the way to go.

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:07.680
ALTERNATIVES:
As you’ve probably noticed by now, this

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:12.880
video was mainly focused at tech roles that
don’t require coding, but these companies

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:15.920
also hire a bunch of non tech roles as well.

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:21.930
For example, they need accountants, data scientists,
product designers, business analysts, recruiters,

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:23.000
and so on.

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:26.190
And these companies pay well for all of these
roles.

00:10:26.190 --> 00:10:32.279
For example, an L6 Google recruiter earns
a whopping $335,000.

00:10:32.279 --> 00:10:37.610
With that being said though, in the end, these
companies are tech companies meaning that

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:42.700
the people who are the most valuable and most
influential will be tech workers.

00:10:42.700 --> 00:10:47.579
This primarily consists of software engineers
and the people who work directly around software

00:10:47.579 --> 00:10:51.570
engineers like product managers, program managers,
and solutions architects.

00:10:51.570 --> 00:10:56.481
So, if you want to play an integral role at
these companies but not have to code, these

00:10:56.481 --> 00:10:59.820
three choices are by far the best options.

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:04.490
If you’re more of a business person and
want to create overarching goals and visions

00:11:04.490 --> 00:11:09.450
and not really deal with the technical stuff,
you might want to look into product management.

00:11:09.450 --> 00:11:13.470
If you’re comfortable dealing with some
technical stuff but still wanna mainly focus

00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:18.339
on the business side of things, you probably
want to look into program management.

00:11:18.339 --> 00:11:22.420
And finally, if you’re super comfortable
with technical stuff, but you just don’t

00:11:22.420 --> 00:11:27.250
wanna do coding specifically, you’re best
bet would be to become a solutions architect.

00:11:27.250 --> 00:11:32.120
All of these roles are in high demand not
just inside FAANG but across the entire job

00:11:32.120 --> 00:11:33.120
market.

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:37.400
In the modern age, virtually every company
needs IT solutions and all of these roles

00:11:37.400 --> 00:11:40.110
are essential in delivering said IT solutions.

00:11:40.110 --> 00:11:44.880
So, even if you aren’t able to crack into
FAANG directly with one of these career paths,

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:48.440
it’s more than likely that you’ll be able
to make it into a background company like

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:50.320
Cisco or Oracle.

00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:55.029
And from there, if you’re truly a star performer,
it’s just a matter of time until you make

00:11:55.029 --> 00:11:58.930
it into a FAANG company if that’s your goal
of course.

00:11:58.930 --> 00:12:01.130
Which role sounds most appealing to you?

